research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-7-{"id":"lem-cauchy-complete-and-archimedean-imply-mct","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5652"],"rationale":"Hall states the equivalence of sequential Cauchy and monotone completeness in Archimedean ordered fields. The local theorem exposes ARCH and supplies a dependency-adapted increment argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-8-{"id":"lem-mct-implies-archimedean","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5652"],"rationale":"Hall defines monotone completeness in the ordered-field setting and places it in the Archimedean completeness equivalence. The local item isolates the standard canonical-naturals contradiction showing MCT forces ARCH.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-9-{"id":"lem-mct-implies-lub","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5652"],"rationale":"Hall states monotone and Dedekind completeness as equivalent for Archimedean ordered fields. The local statement keeps an explicitly redundant ARCH hypothesis and constructs the supremum by bisection.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl:10:{"id":"thm-completeness-equivalences","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5652"],"rationale":"Hall Theorem 3.11 gives equivalence of Dedekind, monotone, Cantor, Bolzano-Weierstrass, and sequential completeness for Archimedean ordered fields. The local theorem repackages exactly the five selected clauses so ARCH is attached only to NIP and CC.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-11-{"id":"def-cesaro-mean","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Summation_methods"],"rationale":"The Encyclopedia of Mathematics defines the arithmetic averages (s_0+...+s_n)/(n+1) and summability by their limit. This matches the local zero-based definition and direction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"thm-cesaro-mean-theorem","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Summation_methods"],"rationale":"The source states Cauchy’s theorem that s_n tending to s implies its arithmetic averages tend to s, with the same quantifiers and conclusion. The numbered head-tail proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-13-{"id":"thm-stolz-cesaro","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"The source gives the infinity-over-infinity Stolz-Cesaro theorem for a strictly increasing unbounded denominator and convergent difference quotient. The local version changes indexing and states the conclusion on a positive tail to admit b_0=0.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
