research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log-8687--- **The companion failure** is [[fs-cauchy-complete-implies-lub]], refuted by the
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log-8688--  same field. Together they are the exact content of the Archimedean hypotheses
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log:8689:-  in clauses 2 and 4 of [[thm-completeness-equivalences]].
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log-8690-+- **The failure is not an accident of one field.** By [[lem-lub-implies-nested-intervals]] every field with (LUB) is Archimedean, so any witness at all must be non-Archimedean; and in a non-Archimedean field the shrinking hypothesis in (NIP) is a severe restriction, because a length that tends to $0$ in the order of the field must get below every infinitesimal. That is why checking shrinking (NIP) in $K$ is substantive, and why $K$ can satisfy (NIP) while failing (LUB) at all.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log-8691-+
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log-8692-+- **$\mathbb{R}(t)$ will not do as a witness**, although it is the library's other non-Archimedean ordered field ([[cex-ordered-field-not-archimedean]]). Nothing in this library establishes any nested interval property for it, and the page that built $K$ says why a new field was constructed rather than reusing that one.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log-8693-+
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub.log:8694:+- **The companion failure** is [[fs-cauchy-complete-implies-lub]], refuted by the same field. Together they are the exact content of the Archimedean hypotheses in clauses 2 and 4 of [[thm-completeness-equivalences]].
