research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-completeness-limits.log-31963-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-completeness-limits.log-31964-- **Why $1/(k+1)$ and not $1/k$.** Sequences here are functions on $\mathbb{N}$ and $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so the index $k = 0$ occurs and $1/k$ would be undefined there. The same convention is used in [[lem-sequential-characterisation-of-closure-r]].
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-completeness-limits.log-31965-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-completeness-limits.log:31966:[L6] (BW) implies the Archimedean property ([[lem-bw-implies-archimedean]]) and so does (MCT) ([[lem-mct-implies-archimedean]]); and the five properties are equivalent once the Archimedean property is supplied where needed ([[thm-completeness-equivalences]]).
